Z||000000||ckphoto@nac.net||10-30-2001||03:45 PM||||Ok I have an 84 2.5 car starts up fine<BR>comes off high Idle after a min. or so<BR>then she seems fine when I take it out and get on it a little and stop the Idle is very high and will drop down after I kick the pedal a few times someone suggested it could be the IAC valve so I cleaned it still same problem so I tried disconnecting it while car is running there is no change in the way the<BR>car runs when disconnected should there be a noticeable change when I unplug it<P>thanks <BR>chris||||reg||5
Z||000001||samoht||10-30-2001||04:28 PM||||try some W-D on that trottle spring, I think it is just sticking||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||10-30-2001||05:30 PM||||Had the same prob with my 84.  First cleaned then replaced the IAC with no change.  Then I changed throttle bodys and replaced the gasket with a new one.  When I took the old one off I found the EGR gas passages all carboned up.  Cleaned these and put the new gasket on and the idle smoothed out a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ckphoto@nac.net||10-31-2001||09:24 AM||||Thanks for the reply guys but its not the <BR>throttle sticking<BR>Should I notice a difference in the car idle when I disconnect the IAC valve if it is functioning properly<BR>thanks<BR>chris||||reg||5
Z||000004||Voytek||10-31-2001||11:20 AM||||If you disconnect it, the computer will pick an arbitrary value, i.e. it may still not run right.<P>If disconnecting the IAC, I would unhook the battery (minimum 15 minutes is what they suggest) to erase all codes and then try again with IAC disconnected to see if there is any change at all.<P>It sounds like that MAY be it, BUT, since it is a sensor, the computer may be storing a code.  I would run the diagnostic (code tester) - real easy and may save you some hassles.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
